{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"arbitrary-allergenic-chameleon","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Where this came from\n\nStep-7 sibling sweep of `smudgeless-friendless-longspur` (`1205-63b2-2c01-4e7f`), whose confirmed\nroot cause is an unbounded external command inside a periodic probe on a `Type=oneshot` unit with\nno start timeout. That combination took Gerrit off the air for 41 minutes. The sweep looked for the\nsame shape elsewhere; these two hits were confirmed semantically (read in context) and are filed\nrather than fixed, because they are outside the blast radius that change reviewed.\n\n## Hit 1 — `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:787`: unbounded `git clone`\n\n```\nif ! git clone -q \"$MIRROR_URL\" \"$MIRROR_DIR\" 2>/dev/null; then\n```\n\nNo bound of any kind, inside the unit driven every 2 minutes by `rebar-autodeploy.timer`. Its\nSIBLING one line down is already correct — `autodeploy.sh:798` wraps `git fetch` in\n`timeout \"$FETCH_TIMEOUT\"` — so the omission is inconsistent within the same function, which is\nthe same signature the 1205 scans had against the `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT` calls in their own file.\n\nPartially mitigated by 1205's fix: `rebar-autodeploy.service` now carries `TimeoutStartSec=90`, so a\nhung clone can no longer delete the timer's next elapse. The clone itself is still unbounded, so it\ncan still consume its whole 90 seconds against a stalling remote on every tick.\n\nThis path runs only when `$MIRROR_DIR` is absent (a fresh box or a wiped mirror), which is why it is\nlower priority than the probe scans were.\n\n## Hit 2 — `infra/scripts/install-certbot-timer.sh:69`: `Type=oneshot`, no `TimeoutStartSec`\n\nRendered with `OnCalendar=*-*-* 00,12:00:00`. Less dangerous than the observability unit because\n`OnCalendar` is absolute wall-clock rather than computed from the last completed activation, so a\nhung run does not delete the next elapse the way `OnUnitActiveSec` does. It still leaves a unit\n`activating` forever with nothing to kill it, and systemd will not start a second activation while\nthe first is running, so certificate renewal silently stops.\n\n## Explicitly NOT filed, with the reason\n\n`bootstrap-gerrit-admin.sh`, `compose-up.sh` and `mcp-entrypoint.sh` also make unbounded `curl` /\n`git clone` calls. They are one-shot bootstrap and container-entry paths, not periodic units, so a\nhang there is visible as a failed boot rather than as a silently missing metric. Different failure\nmode, out of this category. Recorded so the sweep's coverage is legible rather than looking like an\nomission.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - `autodeploy.sh`'s mirror `git clone` carries a finite wall-clock bound, using the same\n      idiom as the `git fetch` beside it.\n- [ ] AC2 - A hanging clone is shown to terminate: the script is driven with a stubbed `git` that\n      blocks, and the run still completes.\n- [ ] AC3 - The rendered certbot service declares a finite `TimeoutStartSec`, asserted against the\n      rendered unit rather than the script text.\n","id":"a008-88cf-056e-44fb","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Two more systemd oneshots can hang unbounded: autodeploy's mirror `git clone` and the certbot unit's missing TimeoutStartSec"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788571774189384721,"uuid":"06849dd8-644f-4adf-bc7c-92fdfe8e6367"}